#{[17592186045463 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/lesson #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045487 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/moderate #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045493 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/radio #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045498 #edb/ref 10 :answer.type/math #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1007 "Mathematics for Machine Learning" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1008 "Our Mathematics for Machine Learning course provides a comprehensive foundation of the essential mathematical tools required to study modern machine learning.\nThis course is divided into three main categories: linear algebra, multivariable calculus, and probability & statistics. The linear algebra section covers crucial machine learning fundamentals such as matrices, vector spaces, diagonalization, projections, singular value decomposition, and regression, as well as dimensionality reduction techniques such as principal component analysis. The multivariable calculus section develops the tools needed for optimization and learning algorithms, including vector and matrix calculus. Finally, the probability and statistics section covers random variables, point estimation, maximum likelihood estimation, and confidence intervals.\nOn completing this course, students will be well-prepared for a university-level machine learning course that tackles concepts such as gradient descent, neural networks, backpropagation, support vector machines, naive Bayes classifiers, and Gaussian mixture models." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1009 "The course begins with foundational topics in set theory and logic. Students develop a precise mathematical language for describing sets, functions, and predicates, and are introduced to key ideas such as supremum and infimum, as well as argmax and argmin notation. Logical reasoning and Boolean functions are also studied, providing important conceptual motivation for understanding the expressive limitations of some simple machine learning models, such as why certain functions (e.g. XOR) cannot be represented by a single-layer neural network.\nStudents then explore matrices in-depth. They study Gaussian elimination, solve systems of equations, learn about determinants and their properties, and compute inverse matrices. These tools form the computational backbone of many machine learning algorithms.\nAs part of this course, students perform a deep dive into vector spaces, exploring linear independence, subspaces, bases, dimension, rank, and nullity. They also study operations such as the Hadamard product, which appears naturally in element-wise computations in neural networks. Students generalize key concepts to abstract vector spaces and inner product spaces, and study orthogonality, projections, and the Gram–Schmidt process.\nStudents will learn how to find eigenvectors of a matrix, compute matrix diagonalizations, and extend these ideas to orthogonal diagonalization of symmetric matrices. These techniques underpin dimensionality reduction methods and are central to understanding the structure of data.\nThe course then develops key applications of linear algebra in machine learning. Students study singular value decomposition and its relationship to principal component analysis, and learn how these tools are used for dimensionality reduction, feature extraction, and identifying structure in high-dimensional datasets. Linear least-squares problems and regression techniques are also studied as foundational methods for fitting models to data.\nA solid grasp of multivariable calculus is essential for understanding modern machine learning algorithms. In this course, students become well-versed in partial derivatives and gradient vectors (used in gradient-based optimization), the multivariable chain rule (essential for backpropagation), and vector-valued functions. Students extend differentiation to maps between multi-dimensional vector spaces and develop the tools of vector and matrix calculus used to compute gradients of loss functions in neural networks.\nStudents also study optimization of multivariable functions, including critical points, second derivative tests, and constrained optimization using Lagrange multipliers. These techniques form the mathematical foundation of algorithms such as support vector machines and constrained optimization problems in machine learning.\nStudents work with standard multivariable surfaces to build intuition for loss surfaces, and study double integrals as a tool for understanding continuous probability distributions.\nOn the probability and statistics side, students study discrete and continuous random variables, including probability density functions, cumulative distribution functions, transformations, expectation, moments, variance, and Bayes' theorem. Important probability distributions are explored in detail.\nStudents extend their understanding to joint, marginal, and conditional distributions, as well as sums and products of random variables. Topics such as covariance, correlation, and multivariate normal distributions are introduced, which are essential in probabilistic modeling.\nThe course concludes with parametric inference, focusing on point estimation, maximum likelihood estimation, and confidence intervals, which form the statistical foundation for many machine learning methods." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1012 145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1013 "MML" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "1ee987ec-c9ff-5d2f-91f4-4de8079c0eac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1031 "Expected Values of Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1033 730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "9438800f-fe1e-5478-b0c5-e3b869cf359e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048508 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048508 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048508 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048508 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048508 #edb/ref 1031 "Equivalent Ratios" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048508 #edb/ref 1033 553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048508 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "a587db09-9578-5d61-b811-99a95c85061d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1031 "Sampling Distributions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1033 3864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "ef34739b-f46e-5073-bcb9-1acc5022fae9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1031 "The Sample Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1033 2980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "57fe5849-4b30-59b2-99bd-4fb2b776d659" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050862 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050862 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050862 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050862 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050862 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050862 #edb/ref 1031 "The Mean of a Data Set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050862 #edb/ref 1033 1634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050862 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6c4b52b3-801d-5a43-b903-ae827a2a786c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156993 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156993 #edb/ref 1071 "A sample $X_1, X_2$ of two metallic rings is drawn from a population. The population has rings of two different diameters, $16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}$ and $\\textrm{18}\\,\\textrm{mm},$ distributed in the ratio $5:1.$ Calculate $\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33bedb30-2c99-52e8-a665-e3bddeed7e13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156993 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu.$ Then, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the rings in the population have diameters $16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}$ and $\\textrm{18}\\,\\textrm{mm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $5:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean, we can consider a population consisting of $5+1 = 6$ rings only with the following diameters:\n\n$$\n16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 18\\,\\textrm{mm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(5\\cdot 16.5+18) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(82.5+18) \\\\  & =\\frac{100.5}{6} \\\\  & =16.75\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu = 16.75\\,\\textrm{mm}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157172 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157172 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n6, \\quad 13, \\quad 7, \\quad 5, \\quad 14 , \\quad 11, \\quad 7\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157172 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a485378b-0bce-5576-bcbc-2ef77df2da17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157172 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu.$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the mean value of $X_1,X_2,\\ldots, X_n,$ while $\\bar{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the value $\\hat{\\mu} = \\bar{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(6+13+7+5+14+11+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{7} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1039 "Estimating a Population Mean From the Sample Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b60ce582-4127-57b8-bba8-04cf17169ca3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1039 "The Expected Value of the Sample Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186156993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "38455e74-50c2-5581-b9e1-a8dd0c85f826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1045 "The Sample Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "44aff9fc-2508-51a9-baf1-3f855c1f0a4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157599 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157599 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143190" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157599 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157599 #edb/ref 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2}$ of two nails is drawn from a population. The population has nails of two different lengths, $6\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{12}\\,\\text{cm},$ distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Calculate $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157599 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ea5fc2c-4d2b-5104-893c-7896d0999b42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157599 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157599 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nWe're told that the nails in the population have lengths $6\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{12}\\,\\text{cm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean, we can consider a population consisting of $3+1=4$ nails only with the following lengths:\n\n$$\n6\\,\\text{cm},\\,6\\,\\text{cm},\\,6\\,\\text{cm},\\,12\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot 6+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(18+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{4} \\\\  & =7.5\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu =7.5\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158230 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158230 #edb/ref 1061 29343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158230 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fab3a24b-2709-54fe-985c-5fe95ec0712c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158230 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158231 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158231 #edb/ref 1061 29347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158231 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a21dbb27-5fb2-54ba-a76f-f7231e67b5fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158231 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158287 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158287 #edb/ref 1061 38136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158287 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c4fadf35-c701-59e2-9c8b-d385cd3565ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158287 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158288 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158288 #edb/ref 1061 38137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158288 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d85b5ed-e218-547b-81cf-36a60c612b6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158288 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158289 #edb/ref 1061 38142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158289 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2edb1e12-aed2-5310-a098-962a8588a4f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158289 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158531 #edb/ref 1064 "In statistics, we often want to use sample data to deduce (or **infer**) the values of various **population parameters**. The two most common population parameters that we wish to find are\n\n- the **population mean** $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the **population variance** $\\sigma^2.$\n\nThe population mean is the mean of the *entire* population. Likewise, the population variance is the variance of the *entire* population. We should consider these values as *fixed* unknowns. \n\nSuppose, for example, we want to find the mean number of pages per book at a particular library. In other words, we wish to find the population mean $\\mu.$ However, it is impractical to count the pages in every book. So, instead, we might approximate $\\mu$ using the following sampling procedure:\n\n- Randomly choose, say, $5$ books off the shelf.\n\n- Count the number of pages in each book.\n\n- Compute the mean of the $5$ observations.\n\nIn this case, the **sample size** $n=5.$\n\nSuppose we follow this procedure and get the following **observations**:\n\n$$\n201, \\qquad 506, \\qquad 58, \\qquad 104, \\qquad 99\n$$\n\nIt's convenient to denote the individual observations as $x_i.$ So, in this case, we have \n\n$$\nx_1 = 201,\\qquad x_2 = 506,\\qquad \\ldots,\\qquad x_5 = 99.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the mean $\\overline{x}$ of our sample as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(201+506+58+104+99) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 968 \\\\  & =193.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n The value $\\bar{x} = 193.6$ is called an **estimate** of the population mean $\\mu.$ We sometimes write $\\hat{\\mu} = 193.6$ to denote that the value $193.6$ is an estimate of $\\mu.$\n\nIt's important to realize that our estimate $\\bar{x}$ depends entirely on this particular sample. We'd get a different estimate for $\\mu$ if we took a different sample." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158531 #edb/ref 1065 4738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158531 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "359539bd-254d-5083-aa76-577b8a159438" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158531 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158560 #edb/ref 1064 "It is helpful to think of the observations of a particular sample\n\n$$\nx_1,\\quad x_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad x_n\n$$\n\nas a particular instance of data generated by a sequence of random variables \n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad X_n.\n$$\n\nLet's make sure we're clear about the notation:\n\n- $X_i$ is a *random variable* that denotes the $i$th observation in a random sample. Its value changes when we conduct a new sample.\n\n- $x_i$ is the value of the $i$th observation in a particular sample.\n\nWe typically assume that our sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ has the following properties:\n\n- They are **independent and identically distributed** (or **I.I.D.**), meaning they are independent and have the same probability distribution. Moreover, they have the same distribution as the population.\n\n- Their mean is equal to the population mean:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}(X_i) = \\mu, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\n- Their variance is equal to the population variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}(X_i) = \\sigma^2, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nWe often refer to a sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ with these properties as a **random sample of size** $\\boldsymbol{n}.$\n\nIn general, if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n,$ then the **sample mean** is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i .\n$$\n\nWe say that the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an **estimator** of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nSince $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are random variables, so is $\\overline{X}.$ The probability distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is called the **sampling distribution** of $\\overline{X}.$ The sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is an important topic we'll discuss in detail in a future lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158560 #edb/ref 1065 6297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158560 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b79ae73d-8ebe-5dda-916d-6eeb61ce850d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158560 #edb/ref 1067 "The Sample Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158561 #edb/ref 1064 "One important feature of $\\overline{X}$ is that it's an **unbiased estimator** of $\\mu.$ This means that the expected value of $\\overline{X}$ equals $\\mu,$ that is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can easily show that $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$ using the properties of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot \\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot \\text{E}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot (\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{E}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{E}[{X}_{n}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot (\\mu +\\mu +⋯+\\mu ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot n\\mu  \\\\  & =\\mu \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fact that $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$ is a desirable property. We'll meet so-called **biased estimators** in future lessons. \n\nFinally, any estimate $\\bar{x}$ of the population mean $\\mu$ found by computing the mean of a sample is called an **unbiased estimate** of $\\mu.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158561 #edb/ref 1065 6298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158561 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4c9ca398-f024-59cf-b484-80cf2f43cc8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158561 #edb/ref 1067 "The Sample Mean as an Unbiased Estimator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412706 #edb/ref 1080 "8.25\\,\\text{cm}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412706 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412706 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2936c8de-b762-59f1-bba9-735fbc48a942" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412710 #edb/ref 1080 "8\\,\\text{cm}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412710 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412710 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "46d59859-24c5-5ddd-ac89-50051b7c27c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412711 #edb/ref 1080 "7\\,\\text{cm}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412711 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412711 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4c983c9b-b809-585e-97ec-8977702a501c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412714 #edb/ref 1080 "7.5\\,\\text{cm}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412714 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412714 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "607fa761-c4b1-5186-83ab-55c0ba40778f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412740 #edb/ref 1080 "8.5\\,\\text{cm}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412740 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412740 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f00c7066-a8f5-5648-b370-55d63c9722ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412745 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412745 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412745 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186412714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412745 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5decded0-8969-541f-936f-5b210c73460d" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
